        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: 房东",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "房东 — fángdōng — \"landlord; landlady\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "我的房东人很好 wǒ de fángdōng rén hěn hǎo — My landlord is a nice person."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The new character, in pieces: 东 (dōng) — five strokes: a bar, a stroke that falls to the left and turns right, a vertical that hooks to the left, then a short stroke falling to the left and a dot. The next lesson puts it in your hand."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "房东 (fángdōng) is built from 房 'house, room' and 东 (dōng) 'east', both inherited from Middle Chinese, bjang and tuwng (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription). How the pair came to mean the owner who lets a room is not settled."
            }
          ]
        },
