          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "hétong is built from hé 'to join' and 同 'together', both inherited from Middle Chinese, hop and duwng (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), which glosses the second 'alike' and 'together'. The dictionary already pairs the two to gloss the first, in the sense of joining; a written agreement is not given there, so how the word came to it is not settled. In the word the second loses its tone."
